WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.800
- So, Australia is running an experiment

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.290
on what happens when you
don't have the equivalent

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:07.860
of the Communications Decency
Act Section 230 on the books.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.470
In Australia, it appears that
the High Court has determined

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.720
the media companies are liable

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.560
for the content that any other user posts

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.890
to their website or Facebook page,

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:18.730
and this is something that a lot

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:20.400
of people think should be
adopted in the United States,

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.910
because they feel that tech
companies have too much power

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.100
and they're silencing
voices, though, to date,

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.200
they've been hamstrung by Section 230

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.830
and the First Amendment
to the U.S. Constitution.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:31.180
Now, I am not an Australian lawyer,

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:32.160
take this with a grain of salt.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.106
I'm doing my best to parse
what I can see coming out

00:00:34.106 --> 00:00:36.300
of the Australian courts,
but at first blush,

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.290
this decision seems to be
the opposite of Section 230

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:41.970
and the media companies might
be liable for these comments,

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.660
so they will be running
a natural experiment

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:44.846
as to what actually happens

00:00:44.846 --> 00:00:47.070
when the internet doesn't
have the equivalent

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.890
of the First Amendment or Section 230,

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.600
and I'm as interested as you
are in finding out what kind

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.230
of unintended consequences, if any,

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:54.880
come as a result of this finding.

